From 4588cd54f65484b99f4c044b5b28880ab28ea077 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 18:32:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Grant access from the shared project instead of a list in
 this repo
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Who may use this app was a hardcoded array of email addresses in js/config.js,
checked in the browser. Inviting a seller meant editing that file and pushing;
revoking meant another commit. And the check was the only one there was, since
the profiles policies asked solely whether a row belonged to the caller — so
any account on this shared Supabase project could create a seller profile here.

Access now comes from the project's app_access table, via has_app_access().
The same grant is checked in two places: the browser, so a refused visitor gets
a clear message, and inside the row level security policies on profiles, which
is the boundary that actually holds. Granting or revoking is a row, and takes
effect on the next page load.

The policies follow the shape used by every app on this project: the app grant,
then either the app_admin role or ownership of the row. Reads widen for an
app admin; writes stay ownership-only, so nobody can alter a profile that is
not theirs.

The browser check fails closed. An unreachable project refuses entry rather
than admitting someone the database would then refuse to serve any data to.

Tests: the harness answers the access RPC so suites can drive the app, and the
invite suite covers a granted account, an ungranted one, and a failed check.
The case that used to assert an empty list left the app open is gone with the
list itself; absence of a grant is never an invitation.

